1
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:36,000
The celebration of a lifetime.

2
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:06,000
Coco is a family, animation, music film directed by Lee Unkrich.

3
00:10:30,000 --> 00:10:36,000
Despite his family’s baffling generations-old ban on music, Miguel dreams of
becoming an accomplished musician like his idol, Ernesto de la Cruz.

4
00:14:00,000 --> 00:14:06,000
Desperate to prove his talent, Miguel finds himself in the stunning and
colorful Land of the Dead following a mysterious chain of events.

5
00:17:30,000 --> 00:17:36,000
Along the way, he meets charming trickster Hector, and together, they set off on an
extraordinary journey to unlock the real story behind Miguel's family history.

6
00:21:00,000 --> 00:21:06,000
Miguel Rivera (voice), played by Anthony Gonzalez.

7
00:24:30,000 --> 00:24:36,000
Héctor (voice), played by Gael García Bernal.

8
00:28:00,000 --> 00:28:06,000
Ernesto de la Cruz (voice), played by Benjamin Bratt.

9
00:31:30,000 --> 00:31:36,000
Mamá Imelda (voice), played by Alanna Ubach.

10
00:35:00,000 --> 00:35:06,000
Abuelita (voice), played by Renée Victor.

11
00:38:30,000 --> 00:38:36,000
Papá (voice), played by Jaime Camil.

12
00:42:00,000 --> 00:42:06,000
Coco is a 2017 American animated fantasy comedy-drama film produced by
Pixar Animation Studios and released by Walt Disney Pictures.

13
00:45:30,000 --> 00:45:36,000
It was directed by Lee Unkrich, co-directed by
Adrian Molina, and produced by Darla K.

14
00:49:00,000 --> 00:49:06,000
Anderson, from a screenplay written by Molina and Matthew Aldrich, and a story developed by
Unkrich, Molina, Aldrich, and Jason Katz, based on an original idea conceived by Unkrich.

15
00:52:30,000 --> 00:52:36,000
The film stars the voices of Anthony Gonzalez, Gael García Bernal, Benjamin Bratt,
Alanna Ubach, Renée Victor, Ana Ofelia Murguía and Edward James Olmos.

16
00:56:00,000 --> 00:56:06,000
It follows a 12-year-old boy in Mexico named Miguel (Gonzalez) who is accidentally transported to the Land of the Dead, where he seeks
the help of his deceased musician great-great-grandfather to return him to his family and reverse their ban on music.

17
00:59:30,000 --> 00:59:36,000
The concept for Coco is inspired by the Mexican holiday Day of the Dead.

18
01:03:00,000 --> 01:03:06,000
Pixar began developing the animation in 2016.

19
01:06:30,000 --> 01:06:36,000
Unkrich, Molina, Anderson and some of the film's
crew visited Mexico for research.

20
01:10:00,000 --> 01:10:06,000
Composer Michael Giacchino, who had worked on prior Pixar
animated features, composed the score.

21
01:13:30,000 --> 01:13:36,000
With a cost of $175–225 million, Coco is the first film with a
nine-figure budget to feature an all-Latino principal cast.

22
01:17:00,000 --> 01:17:06,000
Coco premiered on October 20, 2017, during the Morelia
International Film Festival in Morelia, Mexico.

23
01:20:30,000 --> 01:20:36,000
It was theatrically released in Mexico the following week, the weekend before
Día de Muertos, and in the United States on November 22, 2017.

24
01:24:00,000 --> 01:24:06,000
The film received acclaim for its animation, voice acting, music,
visuals, emotional story, and respect for Mexican culture.

25
01:27:30,000 --> 01:27:36,000
It grossed over $814 million worldwide, becoming the 15th
highest-grossing animated film ever at the time of its release.

26
01:31:00,000 --> 01:31:06,000
Coco received two awards at the 90th Academy
Awards and numerous other accolades.

27
01:34:30,000 --> 01:34:36,000
The film was chosen by the National Board of Review
as the Best Animated Film of 2017.

28
01:38:00,000 --> 01:38:06,000
A sequel, titled Coco 2, is currently in development.

29
01:41:30,000 --> 01:41:36,000
The film explores themes of skeleton, mexico, guitar, afterlife, musician.
